WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.386
Training agents inside of scalable world models.

00:00:04.836 --> 00:00:12.324
This is Dreamer 4, from Danijar Hafner, Wilson Yan and Timothy
Lillicrap at Google DeepMind.

00:00:13.674 --> 00:00:29.154
A world model learns to predict what happens next given what
an agent does. Once you have one, you can train a policy

00:00:29.604 --> 00:00:36.156
The payoff is that the whole loop runs offline. No environment
interaction at any point.

00:00:36.606 --> 00:00:49.710
That matters most where interaction is expensive or unsafe. A
partially trained robot policy in the physical world has to be

00:00:50.160 --> 00:01:00.168
Dreamer 4 is the first agent to obtain diamonds in Minecraft
this way — purely from a fixed dataset, with no environment

00:01:01.518 --> 00:01:05.646
Two families of model exist and neither is sufficient.

00:01:06.096 --> 00:01:18.408
Dreamer 3 and its relatives are accurate and fast, but their
architectures cannot fit complex, diverse real-world

00:01:18.858 --> 00:01:34.362
Controllable video models like Genie 3 scale to diverse video
with diffusion transformers, and generate beautiful scenes.

00:01:34.812 --> 00:01:47.628
And they are slow. Many of them need multiple GPUs to simulate
a single scene in real time, which makes imagination training

00:01:48.978 --> 00:01:53.130
So the engineering target is unusually specific.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:02:04.212
Accurate enough to simulate game mechanics, and fast enough
that a human — or a learning policy — can interact with it in

00:02:05.562 --> 00:02:14.202
The model has two halves. The first is a tokenizer that
compresses raw video into continuous representations.

00:02:14.652 --> 00:02:24.612
It is an encoder-decoder with a bottleneck: the latent tokens
are read out through a linear projection to a small channel

00:02:25.062 --> 00:02:35.214
Crucially it is causal in time. That lets it compress
temporally while still decoding one frame at a time, which is

00:02:35.664 --> 00:02:50.280
It is trained as a masked autoencoder — patches are dropped
with a probability sampled uniformly between zero and zero

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:59.466
The authors report that this masking improves the spatial
consistency of the videos the dynamics model later generates.

00:03:00.816 --> 00:03:06.384
Both halves use the same transformer, and its design is all
about inference cost.

00:03:06.834 --> 00:03:15.882
Dense attention over every video token is prohibitive, so
space and time are factorised into separate attention layers.

00:03:16.332 --> 00:03:26.052
Then a finding that does most of the work: only a small number
of temporal layers are needed. Time attention runs once every

00:03:26.502 --> 00:03:35.790
Grouped-query attention shrinks the KV cache further, which
matters because inference is bound by memory bandwidth as much

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:47.256
Attention is block-causal in time — everything within a
timestep sees everything else in it, plus the past. That is

00:03:48.606 --> 00:03:53.742
The dynamics model is a diffusion model, in the flow-matching
formulation.

00:03:54.192 --> 00:04:06.768
Take a clean data point and a noise sample, and interpolate
between them linearly. The mixing coefficient tau is the

00:04:07.218 --> 00:04:13.626
The network is trained to predict the velocity, the vector
pointing from noise toward data.

00:04:14.076 --> 00:04:27.324
At inference you start from noise and integrate that velocity
field over K steps. Standard diffusion needs 64 steps or more,

00:04:28.674 --> 00:04:37.194
Shortcut models fix the step count by conditioning the network
not just on the signal level but on the requested step size.

00:04:37.644 --> 00:04:42.996
At the finest step size the model is trained with the ordinary
flow-matching loss.

00:04:43.446 --> 00:04:56.094
For larger steps it is trained with a bootstrap loss: take two
half-steps, average their velocities, and use that as the

00:04:56.544 --> 00:05:04.632
The model therefore learns to predict where a large step
should land, rather than accumulating discretisation error

00:05:05.082 --> 00:05:15.498
In practice that turns 64 steps into 2 or 4 — and Dreamer 4
uses K equals four forward passes per generated frame.

00:05:16.848 --> 00:05:26.856
The third ingredient is diffusion forcing, which assigns a
different signal level to every timestep in the sequence

00:05:27.306 --> 00:05:35.538
That means every timestep serves double duty — it is a
denoising target, and it is context for the timesteps after

00:05:35.988 --> 00:05:44.940
It also makes the noise pattern flexible at inference. You can
hold the history clean or lightly noised while generating the

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:58.614
Dreamer 4 uses that: past frames are corrupted to a signal
level of zero point one, deliberately, so the model becomes

00:05:59.964 --> 00:06:10.644
Combining shortcut models with diffusion forcing gives what
they call shortcut forcing. But there is a modification, and

00:06:11.094 --> 00:06:23.334
Shortcut models normally use v-prediction — the network
outputs a velocity. That works well when you generate a whole

00:06:23.784 --> 00:06:32.496
But velocities are high-frequency outputs. Generate a long
video frame by frame and small errors in them compound.

00:06:32.946 --> 00:06:46.026
So they reparameterise to x-prediction: the network predicts
the clean representation directly. The bootstrap term is

00:06:46.476 --> 00:06:59.964
The ablation makes the case: x-prediction and the x-space loss
take FVD from 329 down to 151. It is what enables rollouts of

00:07:01.314 --> 00:07:03.594
One more detail worth a moment.

00:07:04.044 --> 00:07:16.548
At very low signal levels the flow-matching target degenerates
— with almost pure noise as input, the best prediction is

00:07:16.998 --> 00:07:24.678
So they add a ramp weight, linear in the signal level: zero
point nine tau plus zero point one.

00:07:25.128 --> 00:07:39.336
That is the entire mechanism. It moves model capacity toward
the signal levels that actually carry information, and it

00:07:40.686 --> 00:07:46.614
With the world model in place, the agent is built in three
phases on the same network.

00:07:47.064 --> 00:07:56.736
Phase one pretrains the tokenizer on video, then the world
model on tokenized video and, where available, actions.

00:07:57.186 --> 00:08:07.530
Phase two inserts task tokens as a new modality and adds
policy and reward heads, trained by behavioural cloning on the

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:16.356
Phase three is imagination training: reinforcement learning on
rollouts the world model generates itself.

00:08:16.806 --> 00:08:26.022
Nothing is thrown away between phases, and the video-
prediction loss keeps running throughout so the model does not

00:08:27.372 --> 00:08:36.708
Inserting agent tokens into the same transformer creates a
subtle hazard, and the fix is a single line in the attention

00:08:37.158 --> 00:08:45.510
The agent tokens attend to themselves and to every other
modality — images, actions, registers.

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:49.728
But no other modality is allowed to attend back to them.

00:08:50.178 --> 00:09:05.154
Without that, the world model's predictions could be
influenced by which task was requested, rather than only by

00:09:05.604 --> 00:09:12.180
The paper calls this causal confusion, and blocking the
reverse direction is what rules it out.

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:19.098
Imagination training then unrolls the transformer against
itself.

00:09:19.548 --> 00:09:29.292
Each rollout starts from a context taken from the dataset,
then the policy head picks actions and the flow head samples

00:09:29.742 --> 00:09:40.998
The reward head annotates the trajectory, and a value head
predicts lambda-returns by temporal-difference learning so the

00:09:41.448 --> 00:09:49.464
Both reward and value use symexp two-hot outputs, which
handles rewards spanning very different magnitudes.

00:09:49.914 --> 00:10:03.714
Notably the transformer stays frozen. Only the policy and
value heads are updated, which keeps the cost down — and

00:10:05.064 --> 00:10:10.152
The policy is optimised with PMPO, which is deliberately
blunt.

00:10:10.602 --> 00:10:24.402
It looks only at the sign of the advantage, not its size.
States with positive advantage go in one set and get their

00:10:24.852 --> 00:10:37.188
Averaging the two sets separately, with equal weight, means
you do not need to normalise returns or advantages — and no

00:10:37.638 --> 00:10:45.294
A KL term against a frozen copy of the behaviour-cloning
policy keeps it from drifting into nonsense.

00:10:45.744 --> 00:10:53.664
All three terms are measured in nats, which the authors say is
why the balance between them is robust in practice.

00:10:55.014 --> 00:11:06.846
The evaluation is the Minecraft diamond challenge, offline. A
human with experience takes about twenty minutes, which is

00:11:07.296 --> 00:11:19.608
Dreamer 4 clears the early milestones above ninety percent,
reaches an iron pickaxe twenty-nine percent of the time, and

00:11:20.058 --> 00:11:30.666
VPT, OpenAI's offline agent, progresses to sticks and
effectively stops — and Dreamer 4 beats it using a hundred

00:11:31.116 --> 00:11:41.580
The VLA baseline built on Gemma 3 is stronger, reaching iron
pickaxes eleven percent of the time, but Dreamer 4 nearly

00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:53.286
Worth being clear about the headline: zero point seven percent
is a first, not a solution. The claim is that it happens at

00:11:54.636 --> 00:11:58.452
Two things separate out cleanly in the ablation.

00:11:58.902 --> 00:12:14.238
First, behavioural cloning on the world model's
representations beats cloning on Gemma 3's representations,

00:12:14.688 --> 00:12:22.776
Second, imagination training on top of that takes the iron
pickaxe rate from seventeen percent to twenty-nine.

00:12:23.226 --> 00:12:32.946
And the improvement grows with the difficulty of the milestone
— which is the pattern you would want, since easy milestones

00:12:33.396 --> 00:12:40.764
It also reaches each milestone faster, so it is not just more
reliable but more efficient.

00:12:42.114 --> 00:12:53.970
The world model is evaluated separately, and the protocol is
nice: a human player is given a task and a start frame, and

00:12:54.420 --> 00:13:03.924
Lucid-v1 completes zero of sixteen tasks — generations diverge
or interactions are simply ignored.

00:13:04.374 --> 00:13:17.526
The large Oasis model manages five. It fails at building
because after a few blocks it hallucinates whole structures

00:13:17.976 --> 00:13:29.592
Dreamer 4 completes fourteen of sixteen, including placing and
breaking blocks, switching items, fighting monsters, riding

00:13:30.042 --> 00:13:41.034
It does that with a 9.6-second context — six times longer than
prior models — while still running at 21 frames per second on

00:13:42.384 --> 00:13:46.488
This result is the one with the widest implications.

00:13:46.938 --> 00:13:57.570
They train on all 2541 hours of video but provide actions for
only a subset, and measure how well the model follows actions

00:13:58.020 --> 00:14:09.420
With just ten hours of paired actions the model reaches 53
percent of the PSNR and 75 percent of the SSIM of a model

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:16.686
With a hundred hours it reaches 85 percent PSNR and
essentially full SSIM.

00:14:17.136 --> 00:14:29.760
So the world model absorbs most of its knowledge from
unlabelled video, and needs only a small amount of action

00:14:31.110 --> 00:14:38.382
They also test whether action conditioning transfers to parts
of the game it never saw actions for.

00:14:38.832 --> 00:14:51.552
Train with actions only from the Overworld, then evaluate in
the Nether and End — different textures, different blocks,

00:14:52.002 --> 00:14:55.506
The action conditioning largely carries over.

00:14:55.956 --> 00:15:11.196
As far as the authors know this is the first controlled
evaluation of that, and it is the load-bearing evidence for

00:15:12.546 --> 00:15:16.122
The ablation is unusually honest about ordering.

00:15:16.572 --> 00:15:29.148
Start from a diffusion forcing transformer at FVD 306. Simply
cutting to four sampling steps wrecks it — FVD jumps to 875.

00:15:29.598 --> 00:15:38.934
Adding the shortcut model recovers it to 329, so the shortcut
objective is what buys the speed back, not a free lunch.

00:15:39.384 --> 00:15:46.680
X-prediction with the x-space loss takes it to 151. The ramp
weight to 102.

00:15:47.130 --> 00:16:07.722
Then the architecture work — alternating batch lengths,
temporal layers every fourth, grouped-query attention,

00:16:09.072 --> 00:16:22.440
To summarise: the enabling idea is shortcut forcing — shortcut
models combined with diffusion forcing, reparameterised to

00:16:22.890 --> 00:16:35.226
Speed here is a capability rather than a convenience. Four
forward passes per frame and factorised attention are what

00:16:35.676 --> 00:16:42.492
And action labels turn out to be the cheap ingredient, which
points toward learning simulators from web video.

00:16:42.942 --> 00:16:45.534
Four things to hold in proportion, though.

00:16:45.984 --> 00:16:54.408
Zero point seven percent on diamonds is a first, not a solved
task, and the high success rates are on the early milestones.

00:16:54.858 --> 00:17:11.610
The world-model comparison is a human attempting sixteen hand-
picked tasks, judged pass or fail — informative, but a small

00:17:12.060 --> 00:17:22.500
Real-time on one GPU describes inference. Training used
between 256 and 1024 TPU-v5p chips.

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:28.638
And the robotics results are qualitative rollouts rather than
a control benchmark.

